<article class="env-lemma" id="00P0">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00P0">Lemma <span data-tag="00P0">10.116.1</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $S$ be a finite type $k$ algebra which is an integral domain. Let $K$ be the field of fractions of $S$. Let $r = \text{trdeg}(K/k)$ be the transcendence degree of $K$ over $k$. Then $\dim (S) = r$. Moreover, the local ring of $S$ at every maximal ideal has dimension $r$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We may write $S = k[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/\mathfrak p$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00OR" data-tag="00OR">10.114.3</a> all local rings of $S$ at maximal ideals have the same dimension. Apply Lemma <a href="/tag/00OY" data-tag="00OY">10.115.4</a>. We get a finite injective ring map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  k[y_1, \ldots , y_ d] \to S  \]
</div>
    <p>
       with $d = \dim (S)$. Clearly, $k(y_1, \ldots , y_ d) \subset K$ is a finite extension and we win. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>